List<String> trades = List.of("TRD-001", "TRD-002", "TRD-003", "TRD-004", "TRD-005");

// SEQUENTIAL: both are identical — encounter order
trades.stream().forEach(System.out::println);        // TRD-001, TRD-002, TRD-003, TRD-004, TRD-005
trades.stream().forEachOrdered(System.out::println);  // TRD-001, TRD-002, TRD-003, TRD-004, TRD-005

// PARALLEL: forEach is scrambled, forEachOrdered is ordered
trades.parallelStream().forEach(System.out::println);
// Output: TRD-003, TRD-001, TRD-005, TRD-002, TRD-004  ← RANDOM ORDER!

trades.parallelStream().forEachOrdered(System.out::println);
// Output: TRD-001, TRD-002, TRD-003, TRD-004, TRD-005  ← ENCOUNTER ORDER!
// But: intermediate ops (filter, map) still run in parallel
// Only the final forEach step is serialized to maintain order
```

**When to use which:**

```java
// forEach: when order doesn't matter — fire-and-forget side effects
trades.parallelStream()
    .filter(Trade::isSettled)
    .forEach(t -> auditService.log(t));  // log order doesn't matter

// forEachOrdered: when order matters — sequential output
trades.parallelStream()
    .filter(Trade::isSettled)
    .sorted(Comparator.comparing(Trade::getTradeDate))
    .forEachOrdered(t -> report.addLine(t));  // report must be in date order!

// BETTER ALTERNATIVE: use collect instead of forEachOrdered
List<Trade> ordered = trades.parallelStream()
    .filter(Trade::isSettled)
    .sorted(Comparator.comparing(Trade::getTradeDate))
    .toList();  // toList() preserves encounter order AND is parallel-friendly!
ordered.forEach(t -> report.addLine(t));  // sequential forEach on ordered list
```

